# config/Kconfig.mitigations — Threat model and mitigations
#
# Owner decision (wave 11): every mitigation is a switch in `make config`;
# none is forced on. A threat-model profile sets the DEFAULTS of the
# mitigation switches with `imply`, never `select`: each switch can still be
# turned off by hand under any profile. Only mitigations that exist in code
# have a symbol; the ones that do not are listed as comments.

menu "Mitigations"

choice
    prompt "Threat model"
    default THREAT_PHYSICAL_ACCESS if DOMAIN_INDUSTRIAL
    default THREAT_RING3_IMAGE
    help
      Who the image defends against. The choice sets the default of the
      mitigation switches below; it never forces one, and a switch set by
      hand wins. Each profile includes the ones above it. The default is
      the base profile, and "Physical access" for DOMAIN_INDUSTRIAL.

    config THREAT_RING3_IMAGE
        bool "Compromised ring-3 image (base)"
        imply MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX
        imply ML_DATA_SIG_REQUIRED
        help
          Default. An image on the allow-list is compromised and attacks
          the kernel and the other images through syscalls and shared
          hardware. Implies the Spectre v1 index masking and the ML data
          signature check; both are on by default anyway, so this profile
          changes no default.

    config THREAT_THIRD_PARTY_CODE
        bool "Third-party code"
        imply MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX
        imply ML_DATA_SIG_REQUIRED
        help
          Code the owner did not write runs in ring 3 (applications,
          drivers, models). Implies the same switches as the base profile:
          no mitigation specific to this threat exists in the tree yet
          (see the "not implemented" lines below), so today it sets the
          same defaults as the base profile.

    config THREAT_PHYSICAL_ACCESS
        bool "Physical access to the device"
        imply MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX
        imply ML_DATA_SIG_REQUIRED
        imply CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
        imply SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
        imply OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
        imply LINK_AUTH_ENFORCED
        help
          The attacker can reach the console, the removable FAT volume
          (also exposed over USB mass storage) and the boot media. On top
          of the base profile, implies:
            CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN     no kernel console; the ring-3 user
                                 shell is unprivileged unless a signed
                                 topology row grants it more;
            SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED boot halts on an unsigned or badly
                                 signed kernel image ("Security");
            OTA_SIG_MANDATORY    unsigned OTA images are refused;
            LINK_AUTH_ENFORCED   boot halts without a valid
                                 /fat/LINK.KEY instead of running the brain
                                 link unauthenticated.
          Each implied switch carries its own cost and deployment
          requirement; read its help before building with this profile.

endchoice

config MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX
    bool "Spectre v1: mask table indices that come from ring 3"
    default y
    help
      Bounds-check-bypass mitigation, the analogue of Linux's
      array_index_nospec (crates/core/limits/src/nospec.rs). After the bounds
      check, the index used for the load is ANDed with a mask computed
      without a branch (riscv64: sltu+neg; aarch64: cmp+sbc+csdb), so a
      load issued under a mispredicted check reads entry 0, not memory
      past the table. Applied to the syscall table and to the per-task
      tables a syscall indexes with a user value (see the call sites of
      azos_limits::nospec).

      Cost, instructions per operation under -icount
      (tools/vsbench_aarch64.sh, this option y against n, same tree,
      2026-10-02), aarch64/riscv64:
        syscall-floor (getpid, answered before the table)    +0 / +0
        clock [call] (the syscall-table mask alone)          +5 / +4
        chan-call, notify, sensor-read-call (+ cap slot)  +9..10 / +7..8
        ipc-roundtrip                                         +9 / +12
        ring-pingpong                                        +36 / +19
        udp-roundtrip (socket fd masks on every call)        +62 / +140
      aarch64 adds one csdb per masked site (118 in a qemu kernel).

      n: every masked site compiles to the unmasked index.

config ML_DATA_SIG_REQUIRED
    bool "Require an Ed25519 signature on the ML data files"
    default y
    help
      The ring-3 ML service (userspace/services/mlsrv) verifies /fat/MLP.RML
      (MLP weights) against /fat/MLP.SIG and /fat/POLICY.GGF (GGUF
      policy) against /fat/POLICY.SIG before using either: a bare
      64-byte Ed25519 signature over the file's bytes, the same key and
      sidecar format as CAPS.SIG and CONFIG.SIG
      (azos_topology::TRUSTED_PUBKEY; `make` signs both with
      tools/gen_config_sig.py). Both files live on the USB-exposed FAT
      volume.

      A weights file without a valid signature is refused with an
      "[mlsrv] /fat/MLP.RML REFUSED" line and the service serves the
      weights compiled into its own image (digest-pinned like the rest
      of the image), as for an absent file. A refused POLICY.GGF skips
      the GGUF self-test.

      Cost: one Ed25519 verification per file when the service starts,
      nothing per inference. The service image grows by the verifier
      (ed25519-dalek, already in the kernel): 35,576 -> 84,680 bytes on
      riscv64, 25,168 -> 60,928 on aarch64 (exec limit 128 KiB).

      n: both files are used unverified, as before wave 11.

config CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
    bool "Production console lockdown: no kernel console, unprivileged user shell"
    default n
    help
      Under this option the in-kernel shell (the recovery console) is
      not created at all, in safe mode included: boot prints
      "[CONSOLE] lockdown: no kernel console task". The console belongs
      to the ring-3 user shell (SH.ELF, USER_SHELL; with USER_SHELL=n
      the image has no console).

      The user shell's built-in row is reduced: it may start the
      unprivileged tool image (TOOLBOX.ELF) and write /tmp, nothing
      else. It holds no launch right on POWER.ELF (power suspend,
      reboot, shutdown, sched_hz) nor on FLIGHT.ELF, BEHAVIOR.ELF,
      CONFIG.ELF and OTA.ELF: they are refused at SYS_SPAWN_EX and
      recorded. It cannot change /fat. A privileged service shell
      exists only if the signed topology gives an image's row those
      capabilities; nothing on the console can grant them.

      The authority table (crates/core/ipc/src/authority_policy.rs) is
      unchanged by this option for ring 3: a typed syscall checks the
      calling image's own capability on every build. For the recovery
      console it names eight commands: `flight arm` (Cap<Motor>
      WRITE), `pm suspend`, `reboot` and `shutdown` (Cap<Power> WRITE)
      are checked on every build; `behavior disable`, `sched_hz <hz>`,
      `config set watchdog_ms` and `ota rollback` (Cap<Power> WRITE)
      only under this option, and the console would be seeded with no
      capability. Both matter only to a build that still has the
      recovery console, i.e. with this option off.

      Default n: the recovery console exists (it takes the console in
      safe mode, without SH.ELF, or when the user shell is given up
      on), it is seeded with Cap<Motor> and Cap<Power> WRITE, and the
      four lockdown-only rows proceed unchecked. The check's predicate
      also answers "no" during an RFC-0036 containment episode, so
      enforcing those rows on every build would refuse `behavior
      disable` in the middle of one.

      Cost: none on any syscall path; a production image loses the
      in-kernel console as a recovery path.

comment "Not implemented (no symbol until the code exists):"
comment "  branch-predictor / buffer flush on a switch between address spaces"
comment "  kernel page-table isolation (KPTI-equivalent)"
comment "  speculation barrier on syscall entry"

endmenu # Mitigations
